From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Sat, 3 Sep 1994 20:30:19 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-9-28] Cognitive Dissonance: a computational explanation

Akerlof and Dickens[1] explain cognitive dissonance as follows:

"First, persons not only have preferences over states of the world, 
but also over their beliefs about states of the world.  Second,
persons have some control over their beliefs; not only are people
able to exercise some choice about belief given available information,
they can also manipulate their own beliefs by selecting sources of
information likely to confirm 'desired' beliefs.  Third, it is
of practical importance for the appreciation of our theory that beliefs
once chosen persist over time."

I'd like to suggest that, like all other "preference", these
"preferences" about beliefs are based on beliefs, and the
costs and benefits of achieving and using those beliefs.
Indeed, "preference about beliefs" is tautological and useless.
Instead, I would explain as follows: the logical depth[2] 
(computational cost) between old beliefs and new beliefs
via the new information, is higher than the expected value
of the new information.

This would explain most phenomenon normally explained by
"cognitive dissonance", including:
* Resistance to new information that contradicts prevailing beliefs:
first, the value of the beliefs might remain for other circumstances
even though they contradict information in this circumstance, and
second, there can be a large computational cost to reexamining the
origin and consequences of beliefs in order to resolve the conflict.
* Different people interpret the same information differently:
they start with different beliefs and thus different costs of
absorbing information.  Certain classes of information are absorbed
much more readily by people with certain kinds of beliefs, and
this varies widely between different kinds of information.  There
is no belief set which is optimal for absorbing all kinds of
new information.
* Persons who justify to themselves some difficult undertaking
often have a strong and persistant belief that the undertaking
is a good one: this will be true in situations where the cost
of reaching or changing that belief is high (in terms of
cost of absorbing new information).

For how these conclusions might be formalized and [dis]proven, 
see [2].

[1] Akerlof and Dickens (1982), "The Economic Costs of Cognitive
Dissonance", American Economic Review, v72, 307-19
[2] Li and Vitanyi (1993), _An Introduction to Kolmogorov Complexity and 
Its Applications_.

