From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Sun, 4 Sep 1994 16:30:37 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-30] Cognitive Dissonance: a computational explanation

Nick Szabo writes:
>I would explain [cognitive dissonance] :as follows: the logical
>depth[2] (computational cost) between old beliefs and new beliefs via
>the new information, is higher than the expected value of the new
>information.

We of course often don't open info-full junk mail because of just such
an expectation that the value of the info will be less than the
computational cost.

>* Resistance to new information that contradicts prevailing beliefs:
>first, the value of the beliefs might remain for other circumstances
>even though they contradict information in this circumstance, and
>second, there can be a large computational cost to reexamining the
>origin and consequences of beliefs in order to resolve the conflict.

The choice isn't just between ignoring the new info vs. accepting it
and erasing all trace of any conflicting beliefs.  One can also accept
a certain level of contradictory beliefs, or one can reduce the
context of application for the conflicting beliefs until they no
longer conflict.  But these inconsistent or more complex beliefs can
be harder to deal with.  The phenomena of cognitive dissonance tells
us something about the computational value of simple consistent
beliefs, and so may tell us something about the computational
strategies used in human thought.

But computational cost need not be the full explanation for human
resistance to new info.  I can also see evolutionary advantage to
humans who are reluctant to contradict social consensus. 

Robin Hanson

