From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Mon, 5 Sep 1994 00:51:37 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-9-32] Political language

F.A. Hayek and others have pointed out how the use of language has 
so warped our 20th century political dialogue.  (Cf. the
chapter "Our Poisoned Language" in Hayek's _The Fatal Conceit_).
A technique that can sometimes dramatically reveals such warped 
semantics is the substitution of more accurate words in place
of the vague Orwellianisms typically used in our political
discourse.

Homework: for the next week, whenever you read or hear
the words "social" or "public" to refer to things really
controlled by politicans, make the substitution:

warped semantics			more accurate
----------------			-------------
public policy				political policy
public property				politicians' property
Public Broadcasting System		Politicians' Broadcasting System
public university			political university
social workers				political workers
social welfare				political welfare
public financing			tax financing

Using the more accurate language can also make once puzzling phenomenon 
become obvious: why, for example, "public" schools are so much better 
at teaching political correctness than practical intellectual life 
skills.

In many of these cases substituting "bureaucrat" for "politician"
can be appropriate.  "Involuntary" and "tax" can also shed light on 
some concepts described as "social" or "public"; this emphasizes the 
distinction between the coercion inherent in politics and voluntary 
private relationships, rather than the false cloaking of violent
political greed with the mantle of "the public good".

Also, politicians love to use words like "benefits", "health care", 
"welfare", etc. in a context that implies that if they aren't being 
supplied by politicians, then they aren't really being supplied.

Recasting the debate in the more accurate terms of "political vs. 
private", instead of "public vs. private", can quickly cut
through mountains of thick political fog.  

Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

