Subject: [#94-9-33] Cognitive Dissonance: a computational explanation

>Robin Hanson>
>But computational cost need not be the full explanation for human
>resistance to new info.  I can also see evolutionary advantage to
>humans who are reluctant to contradict social consensus. 

It would be interesting to consider cognitive dissonance in the context of
meme dynamics.  This would make it easy to apply evolutionary theory to the
creation and replication as well as survival mechanisms of the memes. 

>Nick Szabo quotes:

>Akerlof and Dickens[1] explain cognitive dissonance as follows:
>
>"First, persons not only have preferences over states of the world, 
>but also over their beliefs about states of the world.  Second,
>persons have some control over their beliefs; not only are people
>able to exercise some choice about belief given available information,
>they can also manipulate their own beliefs by selecting sources of
>information likely to confirm 'desired' beliefs.  Third, it is
>of practical importance for the appreciation of our theory that beliefs
>once chosen persist over time."

>Nick Szabo goes on to say:

>I'd like to suggest that, like all other "preference", these
>"preferences" about beliefs are based on beliefs, and the
>costs and benefits of achieving and using those beliefs.
>Indeed, "preference about beliefs" is tautological and useless.
>Instead, I would explain as follows: the logical depth[2] 
>(computational cost) between old beliefs and new beliefs
>via the new information, is higher than the expected value
>of the new information.

Meme theory would suggest that memes (eg belief complexes) spread in the
population primarily because they are good replicators.  This is not to say
that a component of their replicability is their usefulness to the
individual.  Another component is the meme's ability to survive long enough
in the individual to be able to replicate.  This often implies defensive
abilities within the meme complex that could make it difficult to dislodge
by other memes.  A problem with the cost accounting is that it implies an
independent cost accounter which is itself a meme complex that is
independent of the belief meme.  It suggests that we have a more free
access to the ability to change our belief systems than experience
indicates.

>Nick Szabo>
>This would explain most phenomenon normally explained by
>"cognitive dissonance", including:
>* Resistance to new information that contradicts prevailing beliefs:
>first, the value of the beliefs might remain for other circumstances
>even though they contradict information in this circumstance, and
>second, there can be a large computational cost to reexamining the
>origin and consequences of beliefs in order to resolve the conflict.
>
Again this implies an objective observer cost accountant.  Most religious
belief system memes for example are extremely well defended.  Witness the
way they are inserted into the minds of others (especially the young) using
multiple psychological techniques such as repetition, parental pressure,
use of reward and punishment techniques, continual reinforcement by rituals
and community pressure, etc.  Look at the way they are defended when in
place.  Self referent belief systems generally ploy a faith component that
well defends against assaults by competing memes especially ones that
employ reason over faith approaches.  The changing of these memes takes
much much more than a computational evaluation of the benefits vs. costs of
the change. 


* Different people interpret the same information differently:
>they start with different beliefs and thus different costs of
>absorbing information.  Certain classes of information are absorbed
>much more readily by people with certain kinds of beliefs, and
>this varies widely between different kinds of information. 

Once a belief system is in place this probably correctly describes a
operational dynamic.  Those who believe in miracles have less trouble
believing in this or that miracle.  Persons who believe in scientific
method  have an easier time accepting the results of this or that research.
 
>There
>is no belief set which is optimal for absorbing all kinds of
>new information.

I doubt this is true.  A non-dogmatic open-minded point of view is probably
very superior in respect to being able to absorb and integrate the widest
spectrum of new information.  Although narrow points of view may be better
at absorbing a particular small class of information.

>* Persons who justify to themselves some difficult undertaking
>often have a strong and persistent belief that the undertaking
>is a good one: this will be true in situations where the cost
>of reaching or changing that belief is high (in terms of
>cost of absorbing new information).
>

This can only happen when the proposed undertaking is complementary to the 
meme that is doing the considering.  So far as I can see now this cost
computing meme that has control over the decision to add new memes or
replace older ones is not usually present unless it is attached to a belief
system (metameme) that is complimentary to it.  This meme complex is very
likely to be present in the types of persons who write about Cognitive
Dissonance and also in the readers attracted to it.  Ironically this is a
self fulfilling prophecy exactly like what is described in the theory. 
Unfortunately this type of person is decidedly not in the human majority
and most muddle along under the direction of other meme structures.


